00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:10.100
And so and and this
doesn't just apply to academics. You

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:11.940
know, if you had a career
previously in, like,

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:14.020
nonprofit work or government
work and you know you're

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:16.405
thinking about transitioning to I think
a lot of this is gonna

00:06:16.405 --> 00:06:19.625
apply for you too. And so
what are the big things? Like,

00:06:20.325 --> 00:06:23.625
I think it would be easy
to miss, your soft skills.

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:27.270
I don't use that soft
term dismissively at all.

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:28.870
They are the most
valuable skills.

00:06:28.870 --> 00:06:31.670
And these are things like
the ability to take a vaguely

00:06:31.670 --> 00:06:35.035
scoped problem and turn it
into an act line of research.

00:06:35.815 --> 00:06:38.315
If you finished a PhD,
you know how to do that.

00:06:39.175 --> 00:06:39.895
Other factors,

00:06:39.895 --> 00:06:43.740
like your ability to translate
your expertise to to people who

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:46.480
are intelligent, but don't
share your particular expertise.

00:06:47.260 --> 00:06:48.800
That is a huge,

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:50.540
skill set. You know,

00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:52.800
in order for you to
have influence within your organization,

00:06:53.475 --> 00:06:56.915
you're gonna need to translate
your expertise to leaders and

00:06:56.915 --> 00:06:58.915
other stakeholders
who, you know,

00:06:58.915 --> 00:07:00.995
don't have your
particular background.

00:07:00.995 --> 00:07:04.670
And you got a lot of
practice in public presentation and translation,

00:07:05.530 --> 00:07:10.010
during your academic experience
that most people never get.

00:07:10.010 --> 00:07:12.510
I was never formally trained
in public presentation

00:07:13.195 --> 00:07:16.875
until I got into my PhD
program. And then just out of,

00:07:16.875 --> 00:07:18.795
like, self
preservation, you know,

00:07:18.795 --> 00:07:22.150
your lab members to
protect the, and, you know,

00:07:22.150 --> 00:07:23.510
reputation and
integrity of the lab,

00:07:23.510 --> 00:07:27.370
they're gonna give you a lot of
guided feedback on how to present, well.

00:07:29.275 --> 00:07:30.835
So it's it's easy to get
hung up on, like, oh,

00:07:30.835 --> 00:07:32.955
I I don't know how
to do this particular modeling technique

00:07:32.955 --> 00:07:34.335
in Python, and it's, like,

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:39.040
Those are the hard skills
in the sense of, like, soft

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:41.680
skills, but you can
learn those pretty quick.

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:44.535
You're bringing in a lot of
soft skills that that are very,

00:07:44.535 --> 00:07:46.215
very rare. They're so rare.

00:07:46.215 --> 00:07:48.375
They're not gonna be in the
job posting a lot of times.

00:07:48.375 --> 00:07:51.435
You're gonna need
to proactively plug them

00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:54.420
throughout your resume,
throughout the interview

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:58.520
process. And I promise you
you will see the hiring managers

00:07:59.805 --> 00:08:02.525
perk up when they start
hearing about these really rare

00:08:02.525 --> 00:08:05.985
abilities, that you've had the
last five, six years to develop.

00:08:06.570 --> 00:08:11.050
Yeah. And I I guess to to
add on to that, I think, one,

00:08:11.050 --> 00:08:13.770
there are two things that kind of
come to mind for me here is

00:08:13.770 --> 00:08:17.805
that one is that, expertise
is truly relative. Right?

00:08:17.805 --> 00:08:22.445
Anybody who works in industry
knows this. You easily are the

00:08:22.445 --> 00:08:26.370
expert on wise thing in
your organization because as long as

00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:29.410
you provide as long as you
know more about that than anybody

00:08:29.410 --> 00:08:32.455
else in your organization,
you are the resident expert.

00:08:32.455 --> 00:08:34.855
And I think that, when
I was transitioning,

00:08:34.855 --> 00:08:36.695
that was one thing that
I worried about is, oh,

00:08:36.695 --> 00:08:37.975
I don't know enough.

00:08:37.975 --> 00:08:40.215
But I was just talking to
somebody yesterday and I was

00:08:40.215 --> 00:08:41.080
telling her, like,

00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:44.940
You already know way more about
this industry than anybody I've ever hired.

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:47.160
And ten out of ten times,

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:49.400
I would rather take somebody
who knows a little bit less

00:08:49.400 --> 00:08:52.155
about the industry but has
a proven track record of being

00:08:52.155 --> 00:08:55.035
able to learn very quickly
and can pick it up. Right?

00:08:55.035 --> 00:08:58.075
So I think that's something
that anybody who's in this room

00:08:58.075 --> 00:09:01.240
is clearly interested in
learning and is curious and

00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:03.800
wants to improve their skills. So
I have no doubt about that.

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:06.780
That's kind of the first
thing of, like, expertise is relative.

00:09:07.640 --> 00:09:10.325
And then the second one
is kind of, you know,

00:09:10.325 --> 00:09:13.205
what Joe was saying is
you're trying to translate your skills

00:09:13.205 --> 00:09:15.605
into something that your
audience and people who are

00:09:15.605 --> 00:09:19.720
hiring can understand One of the best
pieces of advice I got was from,

00:09:19.720 --> 00:09:22.920
David Kelly who kind of
established the design thinking

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:25.295
method and and the d
school at Stanford,

00:09:25.295 --> 00:09:27.375
And he really talked
about how, you know,

00:09:27.375 --> 00:09:29.475
every industry and
every organization

00:09:30.175 --> 00:09:33.510
is really just a group
of people who get together and

00:09:33.510 --> 00:09:36.070
kind of decide what
the standards are. Right?

00:09:36.070 --> 00:09:37.990
And you all know
this in data science.

00:09:37.990 --> 00:09:41.110
The standards in data science
have been evolving and shifting

00:09:41.110 --> 00:09:43.525
and what's really
high quality and,

00:09:43.525 --> 00:09:45.445
a cutting edge
has changed a lot.

00:09:45.445 --> 00:09:48.165
And so in order to
actually understand how to translate

00:09:48.165 --> 00:09:50.265
your skills into
what's most valuable,

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:53.680
I personally think one of the only
ways that you can do it is

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:56.800
you have to talk to people and
you have to talk to a lot of

00:09:56.800 --> 00:09:59.495
people to understand how they,

00:10:00.115 --> 00:10:00.835
what, like,

00:10:00.835 --> 00:10:03.635
even the specific words that
they use to describe certain

00:10:03.635 --> 00:10:06.915
skills can be sort of signals
to them that you know or

00:10:06.915 --> 00:10:09.660
understand how
things will work.

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:12.460
And so I remember going
to David Kelly and saying like,

00:10:12.460 --> 00:10:15.360
oh, I'm debating between
these two jobs right now.

00:10:15.895 --> 00:10:20.295
One is, like position a and the
other one is position b. Do you

00:10:20.295 --> 00:10:21.815
think if I take position b,

00:10:21.815 --> 00:10:25.200
it'll make it hard for me to
do position a stuff later on?

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:28.240
And he was like, John, like,
I'm not an expert in this

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:30.320
field, but it's knowable.

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:32.720
And if you don't
understand the answer yet,

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:35.465
it's because you haven't talked
to enough people in the field.

00:10:35.465 --> 00:10:36.905
You need to talk to
enough people to, like,

00:10:36.905 --> 00:10:40.265
wrap your arms around
the norms of that group,

00:10:40.265 --> 00:10:41.645
and then you'll
know the answer.

00:10:42.500 --> 00:10:44.580
I think the takeaway
there for me is, like,

00:10:44.580 --> 00:10:47.460
you need to talk to people
to understand what they value and

00:10:47.460 --> 00:10:50.500
and how the community works,
but also that it's knowable.

00:10:50.500 --> 00:10:53.505
I think a lot of people assume
that You can't get that kind of

00:10:53.505 --> 00:10:55.985
understanding until you've been
working in that industry for

00:10:55.985 --> 00:10:59.105
multiple years, and certainly
your understanding will,

00:10:59.105 --> 00:11:01.540
you know, you'll get a lot
more once you work in it.

00:11:01.540 --> 00:11:04.500
But if you talk to a
lot of people, you can get, like,

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:08.040
eighty, ninety percent of the way
there for a job decision, I think.

00:11:08.635 --> 00:11:11.435
I wanna jump on top of
that one because it's really it's really

00:11:11.435 --> 00:11:12.975
good about talking to people.

00:11:14.235 --> 00:11:18.015
LinkedIn is an incredible tool
for making the transition.

00:11:19.580 --> 00:11:22.940
And maybe, like, I don't know
a lot of people in industry yet.

00:11:22.940 --> 00:11:26.060
I didn't, you know, I didn't
either at one point. And, actually,

00:11:26.060 --> 00:11:29.825
