And so that's that to me
is such a fundamental change in

00:49:44.095 --> 00:49:46.960
how you look at it because
if you're trying to be like, oh,

00:49:46.960 --> 00:49:49.680
should we roll out this change
in our business? Really,

00:49:49.680 --> 00:49:52.000
what a lot of leaders care
about is they just wanna make

00:49:52.000 --> 00:49:54.135
sure that it doesn't
make things worse. Right?

00:49:54.135 --> 00:49:57.815
And so either if it's the
same or better, then that's great.

00:49:57.815 --> 00:50:01.090
But that same result in
academia, if it's the same,

00:50:01.090 --> 00:50:03.970
is seen as like a not
very valuable result.

00:50:03.970 --> 00:50:08.695
And and I think that that
shift is quite fundamental and And

00:50:08.695 --> 00:50:09.655
it's understandable.

00:50:09.655 --> 00:50:11.975
I think that many
people struggle with that shift

00:50:11.975 --> 00:50:12.775
because, you know,

00:50:12.775 --> 00:50:15.815
you're in any organization with
its own values set and things

00:50:15.815 --> 00:50:18.900
like that. You do absorb
that way of thinking,

00:50:18.900 --> 00:50:21.700
and sometimes it needs to
be called out extremely explicitly

00:50:21.700 --> 00:50:25.305
to help people understand how
they were thinking before.

00:50:25.305 --> 00:50:27.945
Because before in academia,
everybody thought this way,

00:50:27.945 --> 00:50:30.105
there was no need to call
it out at all. You know,

00:50:30.105 --> 00:50:32.505
and so sometimes it's hard
to identify when you're actually

00:50:32.505 --> 00:50:34.240
in a different kinda
actual environment.

00:50:35.260 --> 00:50:37.920
I know this time goes
by so quickly. So one

00:50:39.580 --> 00:50:40.080
other

00:50:41.345 --> 00:50:45.105
question I I'd love for us to get
to is I I know a lot of us

00:50:45.105 --> 00:50:48.785
are in a position where
we're hoping to advance our career,

00:50:48.785 --> 00:50:53.310
maybe get the next promotion
and was wondering what advice

00:50:53.310 --> 00:50:57.010
you may have for some of us
who are in that position right now.

00:50:57.635 --> 00:50:59.575
You know, we talked earlier
about, like, networking.

00:51:00.675 --> 00:51:02.775
And I think that, you know,

00:51:06.400 --> 00:51:06.900
promotion

00:51:07.520 --> 00:51:08.720
is often has a,

00:51:10.800 --> 00:51:12.020
has a social component.

00:51:13.120 --> 00:51:16.275
It's not to say it's popularity
contest. It is to say, like,

00:51:16.275 --> 00:51:19.715
you need to be well
aligned with, you know,

00:51:19.715 --> 00:51:22.215
your lead your managers
and leaders about, like,

00:51:23.100 --> 00:51:24.160
what skills,

00:51:24.860 --> 00:51:29.120
what demonstrations are going to
warrant, that promotion. And so

00:51:30.415 --> 00:51:30.815
you know,

00:51:30.815 --> 00:51:33.535
if you're if you're
seeking promotion within your company,

00:51:33.535 --> 00:51:35.295
like, my advice would be like,

00:51:35.295 --> 00:51:37.075
start that as an
explicit conversation.

00:51:37.790 --> 00:51:40.370
You know, with your
manager about, like,

00:51:41.150 --> 00:51:42.590
what are the skills
that, you know,

00:51:42.590 --> 00:51:44.725
you need to see demonstrated,
what are the, you know,

00:51:44.725 --> 00:51:46.085
accomplishments that, you know,

00:51:46.085 --> 00:51:48.645
you need to see so that
it's not like a mystery that arrives

00:51:48.645 --> 00:51:51.385
at the end of the year,
but it's like an explicit, conversation.

00:51:52.650 --> 00:51:54.090
And then, you know,

00:51:54.090 --> 00:51:56.170
you don't have to you don't
have to stop there. Like, you

00:51:56.170 --> 00:51:59.130
know, look around at who
else is getting promoted, you know,

00:51:59.130 --> 00:52:02.015
within your org and, like,
reading between the lines of

00:52:02.015 --> 00:52:02.655
like, okay. Well,

00:52:02.655 --> 00:52:05.215
what have they accomplished
that distinguished them from,

00:52:05.215 --> 00:52:07.555
you know, the rest of
their their peers? And,

00:52:08.335 --> 00:52:09.715
I think that, you know,

00:52:10.860 --> 00:52:13.260
getting aligned on
expectations, but also, like,

00:52:13.260 --> 00:52:16.060
just being a lot of this
just keeps coming back to like being

00:52:16.060 --> 00:52:19.600
very resourceful and self driven
about, like, finding a path

00:52:20.335 --> 00:52:20.835
to,

00:52:21.615 --> 00:52:22.995
to your goals. And,

00:52:23.695 --> 00:52:24.975
you know, if you're
willing to do that,

00:52:24.975 --> 00:52:26.595
I think you have a
lot more opportunity,

00:52:27.535 --> 00:52:29.155
to, to attain promotion.

00:52:30.930 --> 00:52:31.830
Yeah. I think,

00:52:33.250 --> 00:52:36.450
yeah, we we keep coming back to
the same themes over and over, right,

00:52:36.450 --> 00:52:39.425
which is like you need to find
a way to carve your own path to

00:52:39.425 --> 00:52:41.825
some extent when it's not
that clear and and talking to

00:52:41.825 --> 00:52:43.445
people. I think for promotion,

00:52:44.305 --> 00:52:46.640
one story kind of
comes to mind of,

00:52:47.200 --> 00:52:49.840
kind of earlier when I had
first come to industry,

00:52:49.840 --> 00:52:53.455
which is I was a very process
oriented person You know,

00:52:53.455 --> 00:52:56.175
I really believe that if
you set up good processes,

00:52:56.175 --> 00:52:59.455
you basically bias all
of your outcomes towards,

00:52:59.455 --> 00:53:01.295
everything is kind of I
looked at things like very

00:53:01.295 --> 00:53:04.000
probabilistically So it's like I'll
set up a good process.

00:53:04.000 --> 00:53:05.120
That means that, you know,

00:53:05.120 --> 00:53:07.840
the probability that I get
a better outcome is sort of

00:53:07.840 --> 00:53:10.660
determined, in terms of how
you make those decisions.

00:53:11.415 --> 00:53:14.295
And so I used to I spent
my first six months on the job

00:53:14.295 --> 00:53:17.415
just talking all about these
new processes I had implemented

00:53:17.415 --> 00:53:18.715
and how great they were.

00:53:19.255 --> 00:53:22.920
And I have I got feedback
was kind of like we're an outcomes

00:53:22.920 --> 00:53:27.480
driven organization, Jen. Like, all
this talk of process, not,

00:53:27.480 --> 00:53:31.775
like, it's not doing it for
us. And I I remember my first

00:53:31.775 --> 00:53:34.095
reaction was just sort of
like frustration. I was like,

00:53:34.095 --> 00:53:36.115
come on, but process
is so important.

00:53:36.940 --> 00:53:39.920
But I think it kinda goes
to this advice, which is that,

00:53:41.020 --> 00:53:44.240
different organizations will
value different things and,

00:53:44.965 --> 00:53:48.405
every organization is is its
own little fiefdom with its own

00:53:48.405 --> 00:53:51.685
set of values. So you
wanna spend time getting that feedback,

00:53:51.685 --> 00:53:54.460
figuring out what actually
matters and how it talked about

00:53:54.460 --> 00:53:55.900
and how it's framed,

00:53:55.900 --> 00:53:59.260
but I ended up taking a lot
of the same things that I used to

00:53:59.260 --> 00:54:02.800
talk about from a process
perspective, and I just kind of

00:54:03.505 --> 00:54:08.225
framed it and was able to show
how it led to pros to outcome,

00:54:08.225 --> 00:54:11.665
and I would really focus
on talking about the outcomes.

00:54:11.665 --> 00:54:14.830
And it was a total turnaround
in terms of how people

00:54:14.830 --> 00:54:17.870
perceived the contributions that I
made even though it was

00:54:17.870 --> 00:54:19.390
the same kind of work.

00:54:19.390 --> 00:54:21.875
Right? So I think That's
one thing is, like,

00:54:21.875 --> 00:54:24.115
figure out what
matters to the org.

00:54:24.115 --> 00:54:27.235
And then the second thing
I would say about promotion is

00:54:27.235 --> 00:54:31.360
that increasingly, I think
at some levels, you know,

00:54:31.360 --> 00:54:32.880
depending on your track,

00:54:32.880 --> 00:54:37.415
the promotions end up being like similar
work but also at a certain point,

00:54:37.415 --> 00:54:40.455
there's some promotions that lead
to jobs that have like

00:54:40.455 --> 00:54:43.175
different it's like a
different job. Right?

00:54:43.175 --> 00:54:43.770
Like,

00:54:43.770 --> 00:54:46.890
I kind of at some point was an
IC and went to a manager and it

00:54:46.890 --> 00:54:48.410
was ended up being a
very different job.

00:54:48.410 --> 00:54:51.770
And so one last thing I I kind
of wanted to add to add here is

00:54:51.770 --> 00:54:57.645
that increasingly now I am kind
of, I increasingly, I'm I'm

00:54:57.645 --> 00:54:59.245
